## Nutrient guidance

### Sodium (salt)
- General limit: under 2,300 mg a day, about 1 teaspoon of salt from all food and drink. Many people with CKD need less, as set by their care team. [kidney.org G4] [NIDDK] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen] [Rogosin]
- Check labels. A % Daily Value of 20% or more is high. Foods that don't taste salty can be high in sodium. [NIDDK]
- Rinse canned vegetables, beans, meats and fish. Choose "no salt added." [NKFM] [NIDDK]
- Season with herbs, spices, garlic, lemon juice. [NKFM] [Rogosin] [NIDDK]

### Potassium
- No general numeric limit in these sources. Limits are set from blood tests. [Rogosin] [Kidney Fund: stage 4] [Kidney Fund: eating plan]
- Limit large portions of high-potassium foods (bananas, oranges, grapefruit, melons, potatoes, tomatoes, spinach, avocado, prunes, raisins, winter squash). [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [Rogosin] [NIDDK]
- Lower-potassium choices: apples, berries, grapes, pineapple, cauliflower, onions, peppers, lettuce, cabbage, green beans, white rice, white bread. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [NKFM]
- Drain and rinse canned fruits and vegetables and discard the liquid. [NIDDK]
- Avoid salt substitutes that contain potassium chloride. [NKFM] [Rogosin] [NIDDK] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen]
- If you have diabetes and low blood sugar, apple, grape or cranberry juice is lower in potassium than orange juice. [NIDDK]

### Phosphorus
- No general numeric limit in these sources. Limits are set from blood tests, and a phosphate binder may be prescribed. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [NIDDK]
- Avoid ingredients listing "phos" or "phosphorus" (processed meats, colas, many packaged foods). [NKFM] [NIDDK]
- Animal-source phosphorus is absorbed more easily than plant-source phosphorus. [NIDDK] [Rogosin]
- Lower-phosphorus choices: white bread, Italian or sourdough bread, corn or rice cereals, cream of wheat, unsalted popcorn, light-colored sodas and lemonade. [Kidney Fund: eating plan]
- Higher-phosphorus foods to limit: whole-grain bread, bran cereal, oatmeal, nuts, seeds, dark colas, high-phosphorus cheeses. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [kidney.org superfoods]

### Protein
- Amount set by his doctor or dietitian. Too little or too much can both cause problems. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen] [NIDDK]
- General references in the sources range from about 0.6 to 1 g/kg of body weight a day. [Rogosin] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen]
- Favor lean protein: eggs, skinless chicken or turkey, fish, beans, lentils, tofu. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen] [kidney.org superfoods]
- Eat red meat and organ meats less often, about once a week at most. [Rogosin] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen]
- Keep meat portions small. Fill more of the plate with vegetables. [Kidney Fund: Kitchen]

### Fluids
- Some people with CKD need to limit fluids. Ask his care team for a daily target. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [NIDDK] [Kidney Fund: stage 4] 
- Anything that is liquid at room temperature counts: water, coffee, milk, juice, soup, ice, gelatin, ice cream. [Kidney Fund: eating plan] [Kidney Fund: Kitchen]
- Tips for thirst: sugar-free hard candy, chewing gum, small cups, limit salt. [Kidney Fund: eating plan]

### Fats and sugar
- Choose unsaturated fats: olive oil, canola oil, avocado, nuts and seeds (portions matter because of phosphorus). [NIDDK] [Rogosin]
- Limit saturated and trans fats: red meat, butter, fried and fast food, many packaged baked goods. [NIDDK] [Kidney Fund: eating plan]
- Limit sweets and sugary drinks. [Rogosin] [Kidney Fund: eating plan]

